\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s10 \li1209 \fi-1007 \ri1007 \ql Class Description\
\fs14\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 NSTimer creates timer objects, or more simply, \i timers\i0 . A timer waits until a certain time interval has elapsed and then fires, sending a specified message to a specified object. For example, you could create an NSTimer that sends a message to a window, telling it to update itself after a certain time interval.\
\fs14\
\fs28 Timers work in conjunction with NSRunLoop objects. NSRunLoops control loops that wait for input, and they use timers to help determine the maximum amount of time they should wait. When the timer\'27s time limit has elapsed, the NSRunLoop fires the timer (causing its message to be sent), then checks for new input.\
\fs14\
\fs28 There are several ways to create a timer. The \b scheduledTimerWithTimeInterval\b0 ... class methods automatically register the new timer with the current NSRunLoop object in the default mode (NSDefaultRunLoopMode). The \b timerWithTimeInterval\b0 ... class methods create timers that you may register at a later time by sending the message \b addTimer:forMode:\b0  to the NSRunLoop. If you specify that the timer should repeat, it will automatically reschedule itself after it fires. \
\fs14\
\fs28 There is no method that removes the association of a timer from an NSRunLoop\'d0send the timer the \b invalidate\b0  message instead. \b invalidate\b0  disables the timer, so it will no longer affect the NSRunLoop.\
\fs14\
\fs28 See the NSRunLoop class description for more information on NSRunLoops.\
\fs14\
